00:04:57.352 --> 00:04:59.105
- You read to, you give them a bath,

00:04:59.105 --> 00:05:01.638
you put them in their
PJs, you read to them,

00:05:01.638 --> 00:05:02.717
you kind of transition,

00:05:02.717 --> 00:05:04.818
it's like the Goodnight Moon,

00:05:04.818 --> 00:05:06.956
it's a goodnight for everything

00:05:06.956 --> 00:05:09.920
which is really a demarcation line

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.477
between your day with all its stresses,

00:05:12.477 --> 00:05:14.782
however blessed our lives may be,

00:05:14.782 --> 00:05:17.699
every day has stresses, challenges,

00:05:19.995 --> 00:05:22.782
difficult things and incompletions.

00:05:22.782 --> 00:05:25.580
There's nobody who has any interesting job

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:26.913
or is a busy mom

00:05:28.478 --> 00:05:30.203
who finishes the day and says,

00:05:30.203 --> 00:05:32.175
"I did absolutely everything."

00:05:32.175 --> 00:05:33.849
- [Interviewer] Yeah, there's
always something that--

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:37.018
- That was on my list, I should have done.

00:05:37.018 --> 00:05:38.575
Never.

00:05:38.575 --> 00:05:43.440
So you need to somehow tell
your brain and your body,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.871
we did what we did this day

00:05:45.871 --> 00:05:49.436
and now the day has come to a close

00:05:49.436 --> 00:05:51.269
and we need to fully--

00:05:53.254 --> 00:05:54.487
- Cross the line.
- Recharge.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:55.944
We cross the line.

00:05:55.944 --> 00:05:57.472
We need to cross that line.

00:05:57.472 --> 00:05:58.902
- [Interviewer] Not be
in bed, on the screen,

00:05:58.902 --> 00:06:00.089
thinking about work--
- Yeah.

00:06:00.089 --> 00:06:01.987
- [Interviewer] And then try
to sleep at the same time.

00:06:01.987 --> 00:06:04.844
Couldn't cut off--
- I just really,

00:06:04.844 --> 00:06:07.962
I wish I could sort of convey that feeling

00:06:07.962 --> 00:06:10.255
that I absolutely totally believe now

00:06:10.255 --> 00:06:11.544
to everybody watching

00:06:11.544 --> 00:06:13.498
because I promise

00:06:13.498 --> 00:06:15.081
that if you do this

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:19.199
every aspect of your life will improve.

00:06:20.253 --> 00:06:21.925
If you have any lingering doubt

00:06:21.925 --> 00:06:23.701
that you're not going to be as productive,

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:25.776
just try it.
- Yeah.

00:06:25.776 --> 00:06:28.281
- Because it's like getting in the Tesla.

00:06:28.281 --> 00:06:30.554
You're going to get to
your destination faster.

00:06:30.554 --> 00:06:31.426
- Mhmm.
- Right?

00:06:31.426 --> 00:06:32.468
- Smoother.
- Smoother!

00:06:32.468 --> 00:06:33.301
- [Interviewer] Cleaner.

00:06:33.301 --> 00:06:36.955
- With the kind of, consuming less energy.

00:06:36.955 --> 00:06:40.554
- In that hour to 90
minutes prior to sleep,

00:06:40.554 --> 00:06:43.484
dim the lights in your house,

00:06:43.484 --> 00:06:45.738
don't look at any screens anymore,

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.262
that means no TV and no handhelds.

00:06:48.262 --> 00:06:50.147
You're not Twittering and Facebooking

00:06:50.147 --> 00:06:52.445
and doing all the other
things on your phone,

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:54.388
and I really mean, no texting either.

00:06:54.388 --> 00:06:56.764
Just stop in that last hour.

00:06:56.764 --> 00:06:58.527
That means if you want to read a book,

00:06:58.527 --> 00:07:00.457
go ahead, read a book.

00:07:00.457 --> 00:07:04.035
Don't read magazines with
dozens and dozens of pictures

00:07:04.035 --> 00:07:08.009
to start dropping dopamine in
the back of your brain stem.

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:11.791
Just read one thing, read
one article or one book

00:07:11.791 --> 00:07:14.799
or journal and plan your day,

00:07:14.799 --> 00:07:16.549
with no noise, no TV.

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.746
I mean I know you got,
you might have the kids,

00:07:19.746 --> 00:07:21.267
and they might be loud and everything else

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:22.915
but then turn the TV out for yourself.

00:07:22.915 --> 00:07:23.965
And you're like, "But Brendon,

00:07:23.965 --> 00:07:25.891
"I got to fall asleep with the TV."

00:07:25.891 --> 00:07:28.593
No you don't, you just taught yourself to.

00:07:28.593 --> 00:07:31.330
Trust me, thousands and thousands of years

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:34.485
of human history passed
with no television.

00:07:34.485 --> 00:07:36.176
They slept just fine.

00:07:36.176 --> 00:07:37.747
I'm sure if you ever gone camping

00:07:37.747 --> 00:07:39.094
or been in the great outdoors,

00:07:39.094 --> 00:07:40.347
you're like wow I sleep amazing!

00:07:40.347 --> 00:07:43.253
It's because you're not
watching television I hope.

00:07:43.253 --> 00:07:45.433
Now people, they're in the campgrounds,

00:07:45.433 --> 00:07:46.589
watch in their iPads.

00:07:46.589 --> 00:07:49.935
It's like, you got to turn it off,

00:07:49.935 --> 00:07:53.715
dim the lights, no screens, no sounds.

00:07:53.715 --> 00:07:55.608
Just chill the last hour

00:07:55.608 --> 00:07:58.901
and allow you to kind of
glide yourself into bed.

00:07:58.901 --> 00:08:01.557
Calm, cool, you'll sleep like a champ.

00:08:01.557 --> 00:08:04.217
- Tip number three is cool it down.

00:08:04.217 --> 00:08:08.050
I sleep in as cold an
environment as possible.

00:08:08.948 --> 00:08:10.246
I sleep with less blankets.

00:08:10.246 --> 00:08:11.988
I don't wear any clothes

00:08:11.988 --> 00:08:14.243
and I turn on the air-conditioning down

00:08:14.243 --> 00:08:15.910
to around 69, 70 max

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:19.313
and I generally will
have a fan blowing on me.

00:08:19.313 --> 00:08:22.754
The cooler my environment, the
better sleep I actually get.

00:08:22.754 --> 00:08:25.963
Research has shown that this
is an incredibly powerful

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:28.222
and effective way to enhance yours.

00:08:28.222 --> 00:08:29.697
When you're hot, when you're sweating,

00:08:29.697 --> 00:08:32.458
when you're sleeping with all
those comfy cozy blankets.

00:08:32.458 --> 00:08:35.624
When you're snuggling
with your sweetie, do it.

00:08:35.624 --> 00:08:37.680
But when you want solid
sleep, get rid of it.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.749
- How to increase the quality of sleep?

00:08:39.749 --> 00:08:43.563
Don't try to increase
the quality of sleep.

00:08:43.563 --> 00:08:45.746
The ideal way to live is

00:08:45.746 --> 00:08:48.900
there is no sleep in my life.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.221
But that's not possible.

00:08:50.221 --> 00:08:53.590
Right now, still body has some inertia

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.544
so it sleeps minimum,

00:08:55.544 --> 00:08:58.602
rest at the time it is at ease.

00:08:58.602 --> 00:09:00.602
If you keep this at ease

00:09:01.599 --> 00:09:04.601
then sleep does not occur to the body

00:09:04.601 --> 00:09:09.091
unless it comes to a
certain point of exhaustion.

00:09:09.091 --> 00:09:12.880
So body is never ever asking for sleep

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:16.449
but it is definitely asking for rest.

00:09:16.449 --> 00:09:20.030
If you do not know how to
sit here in a restful manner,

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.363
it'll try to rest like this.

00:09:25.345 --> 00:09:28.345
If you can sit here totally at ease,

00:09:29.378 --> 00:09:33.378
it'll sit here for hours
without falling asleep.

00:09:34.883 --> 00:09:39.050
Otherwise this is the only
way it knows how to rest.

00:09:40.528 --> 00:09:43.685
So do not try to increase
the quality of sleep.

00:09:43.685 --> 00:09:45.490
There's no such thing.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.116
Just learn to keep your body restful

00:09:48.116 --> 00:09:50.456
and at ease consciously.

00:09:50.456 --> 00:09:51.733
If you sit here now,

00:09:51.733 --> 00:09:53.260
one simple thing you can do is

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:55.825
you sit down, you come and sit down here,

00:09:55.825 --> 00:09:59.097
just move your attention
from the top of your head

00:09:59.097 --> 00:10:00.838
down to your toes and see if everything is

00:10:00.838 --> 00:10:03.953
loose and breezy and relaxed and at ease

00:10:03.953 --> 00:10:05.953
unless you're like this.

00:10:09.159 --> 00:10:13.037
Just bring this to ease
as much as you can.

00:10:13.037 --> 00:10:15.812
Not everything is in your conscious level.

00:10:15.812 --> 00:10:19.574
As much as you can
consciously bring it to ease.

00:10:19.574 --> 00:10:21.630
You will see, suddenly if you are the kind

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:23.438
who'll fall asleep at 10 o'clock,

00:10:23.438 --> 00:10:26.442
if you simply, one hour
you sit here at ease,

00:10:26.442 --> 00:10:29.446
you will see your sleep will
get postponed by half an hour.

00:10:29.446 --> 00:10:31.438
You won't feel sleepy at 10.

00:10:31.438 --> 00:10:34.047
Naturally you're awake.

00:10:34.047 --> 00:10:38.635
So instead of trying to
increase the sleep quality,

00:10:38.635 --> 00:10:41.718
no, you increase the quality of life.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.603
If you increase the quality of life,

00:10:45.603 --> 00:10:48.159
when I say increasing the quality of life,

00:10:48.159 --> 00:10:51.853
nothing in your life,
whatever you may be doing,

00:10:51.853 --> 00:10:54.476
from simple breathing
to any complex activity

00:10:54.476 --> 00:10:56.120
that you do in your life,

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.836
the quality of that will not improve

00:10:58.836 --> 00:11:01.154
if you cannot do it with ease.

00:11:01.154 --> 00:11:02.071
Is that so?

00:11:03.895 --> 00:11:04.728
Hmm?

00:11:05.562 --> 00:11:09.382
Only when you can do
something with total ease,

00:11:09.382 --> 00:11:11.116
let's say you're riding a bicycle.

00:11:11.116 --> 00:11:12.477
If you're riding like this, like this,

00:11:12.477 --> 00:11:15.184
you can't say you're
enjoying your cycling.

00:11:15.184 --> 00:11:16.844
If you can ride it with ease,

00:11:16.844 --> 00:11:17.677
it's so much easeier

00:11:17.677 --> 00:11:19.453
that even if you take off your hands,

00:11:19.453 --> 00:11:22.967
it will go only straight
the way you want it to go.

00:11:22.967 --> 00:11:24.174
You're at ease,

00:11:24.174 --> 00:11:26.316
because you are at ease

00:11:26.316 --> 00:11:29.815
the quality of your cycling
has improved, isn't it?

00:11:29.815 --> 00:11:32.341
So these two things are connected.

00:11:32.341 --> 00:11:35.591
So if you enhance the
quality of your life,

00:11:35.591 --> 00:11:38.508
naturally sleep quota will go down.

00:11:39.463 --> 00:11:42.630
If your quality of your life goes bad,

00:11:43.749 --> 00:11:45.623
you can't do anything with these

00:11:45.623 --> 00:11:47.786
then also you cannot sleep

00:11:47.786 --> 00:11:50.708
but stressfully you cannot sleep,

00:11:50.708 --> 00:11:53.644
not because you're well
rested, you cannot sleep.

00:11:53.644 --> 00:11:56.413
You cannot sleep because
you're stressed out,

00:11:56.413 --> 00:12:00.107
if you do this you will not live long.

00:12:00.107 --> 00:12:03.967
If your body does not get enough rest,

00:12:03.967 --> 00:12:07.401
it will die, it will
break, something will go.

00:12:07.401 --> 00:12:11.568
If you are not at ease that
means you are in dis-ease,

00:12:14.088 --> 00:12:15.253
getting there.

00:12:15.253 --> 00:12:17.336
You are in dis-ease means

00:12:18.708 --> 00:12:20.994
you're in a certain state of ill health

00:12:20.994 --> 00:12:22.946
but if we say you're deceased

00:12:22.946 --> 00:12:24.022
that means you're dead.

00:12:24.022 --> 00:12:26.512
(laughing)

00:12:26.512 --> 00:12:29.575
So don't move and stay into
the direction of dis-ease.

00:12:29.575 --> 00:12:32.875
Move into the direction of ease.

00:12:32.875 --> 00:12:35.017
If you become totally ease,

00:12:35.017 --> 00:12:38.821
sleep quality, sleep quota will come down.

00:12:38.821 --> 00:12:40.664
Quality of sleep will be good

00:12:40.664 --> 00:12:42.599
because quality of life is good.

00:12:42.599 --> 00:12:43.915
- The last thing we have to talk about

00:12:43.915 --> 00:12:45.851
is improving your sleep environment

00:12:45.851 --> 00:12:47.231
and that'll involves your bed itself,

00:12:47.231 --> 00:12:48.307
the amount of light in your room,

00:12:48.307 --> 00:12:50.140
the sounds, the odors, the temperature,

00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:51.715
a lot of different factors.

00:12:51.715 --> 00:12:53.086
So we're going to briefly go through

00:12:53.086 --> 00:12:54.664
a few of them here and while we do that

00:12:54.664 --> 00:12:56.043
for each one ask yourself

00:12:56.043 --> 00:12:57.960
if you could be making any improvements.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.089
So first off, let's
talk about your pillow.

00:13:00.089 --> 00:13:01.288
A couple of things about pillows,

00:13:01.288 --> 00:13:04.266
number one, you want to replace
yours every couple of years,

00:13:04.266 --> 00:13:05.793
both because it breaks down over time

00:13:05.793 --> 00:13:06.849
and gets less supportive

00:13:06.849 --> 00:13:09.114
but also because every
night you're sleeping on it

00:13:09.114 --> 00:13:10.791
you're kind of injecting that pillow

00:13:10.791 --> 00:13:12.713
with lots of dirt and germs and body odor

00:13:12.713 --> 00:13:14.113
and stuff that makes it gross

00:13:14.113 --> 00:13:15.979
which can make it hard to fall asleep.

00:13:15.979 --> 00:13:18.650
Secondly, and this was news
to me until just recently,

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:19.983
there are different kinds of pillows

00:13:19.983 --> 00:13:21.781
for different kinds of sleepers.

00:13:21.781 --> 00:13:23.354
For example, I am a side sleeper

00:13:23.354 --> 00:13:24.295
and for years

00:13:24.295 --> 00:13:27.045
I was just using the most
non-supportive awful pillows

00:13:27.045 --> 00:13:29.269
until I learned that a
side sleeper probably

00:13:29.269 --> 00:13:31.061
is going to need a firmer pillow

00:13:31.061 --> 00:13:33.300
that has these gussets on the side of it

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.083
instead of just the two pieces of fabric

00:13:35.083 --> 00:13:36.691
that are stapled directly together.

00:13:36.691 --> 00:13:39.564
And after sleeping on this
thing for about a week now

00:13:39.564 --> 00:13:41.808
I can tell you, it is a lot better

00:13:41.808 --> 00:13:43.370
than the stuff I was using before.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:44.939
When it comes to the
temperature in your room,

00:13:44.939 --> 00:13:46.040
cooler is better.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:47.956
And according to the
National Sleep Foundation,

00:13:47.956 --> 00:13:49.440
the optimal temperature range for sleeping

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.125
is somewhere between 60 degrees Fahrenheit

00:13:51.125 --> 00:13:53.230
and 67 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:54.442
The amount of light in your room

00:13:54.442 --> 00:13:56.074
or should I say lack of light

00:13:56.074 --> 00:13:57.361
plays a big role as well

00:13:57.361 --> 00:13:58.552
because pitch darkness

00:13:58.552 --> 00:14:01.518
actually plays a big role
in melatonin secretion.

00:14:01.518 --> 00:14:03.690
So if you have a lot of ambient
light coming into your room

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:05.386
from like a streetlight
outside your apartment

00:14:05.386 --> 00:14:06.865
you can pick up a blackout curtain

00:14:06.865 --> 00:14:09.005
which will basically eliminate
that light altogether.

00:14:09.005 --> 00:14:10.316
I actually got one right there

00:14:10.316 --> 00:14:11.515
that I use for filming.

00:14:11.515 --> 00:14:13.525
Also if you happen to
fall asleep with the TV on

00:14:13.525 --> 00:14:14.944
you should probably
try to break that habit

00:14:14.944 --> 00:14:16.757
because well blue light.

00:14:16.757 --> 00:14:18.013
Now if you're one of those people

00:14:18.013 --> 00:14:19.452
who thinks that the TV is soothing

00:14:19.452 --> 00:14:21.168
and they just can't
fall asleep without it,

00:14:21.168 --> 00:14:23.507
there are some sound based alternatives.

00:14:23.507 --> 00:14:25.751
And actually silence really
isn't the optimal solution

00:14:25.751 --> 00:14:26.617
for all people

00:14:26.617 --> 00:14:29.425
because silence is so often inconsistent.

00:14:29.425 --> 00:14:30.638
There's a bump in the night

00:14:30.638 --> 00:14:32.096
or there's a voice in the other room

00:14:32.096 --> 00:14:34.261
or there's somebody snoring
in the same room as you.

00:14:34.261 --> 00:14:35.817
So having some sound

00:14:35.817 --> 00:14:37.611
that can block out those inconsistencies

00:14:37.611 --> 00:14:38.942
can really help you fall asleep.

00:14:38.942 --> 00:14:40.098
One option you can check out

00:14:40.098 --> 00:14:41.644
is ambient noise generators

00:14:41.644 --> 00:14:43.455
which can generate both white noise

00:14:43.455 --> 00:14:45.253
which is basically just consistent noise

00:14:45.253 --> 00:14:47.310
that sounds a little bit
like an air conditioner

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:49.421
and ambient noises like rain and thunder.

00:14:49.421 --> 00:14:52.299
And for me, my favorite one is Noisli

00:14:52.299 --> 00:14:54.164
because you can add as many as you want.

00:14:54.164 --> 00:14:55.827
You can change the volume of each one

00:14:55.827 --> 00:14:58.312
and basically create
the perfect mix for you.

00:14:58.312 --> 00:14:59.572
There's also lots of music out there

00:14:59.572 --> 00:15:00.844
that's great for falling asleep too.

00:15:00.844 --> 00:15:01.925
I've made a playlist of my own

00:15:01.925 --> 00:15:03.437
and found several on Spotify

00:15:03.437 --> 00:15:04.806
and I'll link to those down below.

00:15:04.806 --> 00:15:08.219
But my favorite way to fall
asleep is with audiobooks.

00:15:08.219 --> 00:15:09.960
For me at least, there is nothing better

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.730
to fall asleep to than spoken word

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:13.415
and in fact ever since
I discovered audiobooks

00:15:13.415 --> 00:15:15.536
back when I was probably 12 or 13,

00:15:15.536 --> 00:15:17.468
all the way up until I
graduated high school

00:15:17.468 --> 00:15:18.772
and got a roommate in college,

00:15:18.772 --> 00:15:20.379
I would fall asleep every single night

00:15:20.379 --> 00:15:22.402
to either Ender's Game by Orson Scott Card

00:15:22.402 --> 00:15:24.856
or The Hitchhiker's Guide to
the Galaxy by Douglas Adams.

00:15:24.856 --> 00:15:28.082
And as a result, I have huge
swathes of both of those books

00:15:28.082 --> 00:15:29.352
basically memorized.

00:15:29.352 --> 00:15:30.726
- For me the most effective strategy

00:15:30.726 --> 00:15:32.535
that I found for sleeping better

00:15:32.535 --> 00:15:34.106
is having a night time routine.

00:15:34.106 --> 00:15:35.144
I think we all have

00:15:35.144 --> 00:15:37.267
some kind of daily routine in the morning

00:15:37.267 --> 00:15:38.738
what you do when you first wake up

00:15:38.738 --> 00:15:39.571
but we don't necessarily

00:15:39.571 --> 00:15:41.087
have a great night time routine

00:15:41.087 --> 00:15:42.574
to help you unwind.

00:15:42.574 --> 00:15:45.487
And so it changes as my life changes

00:15:45.487 --> 00:15:48.633
but just like when a child
is getting ready for bed

00:15:48.633 --> 00:15:50.439
you'll have them get into their pajamas

00:15:50.439 --> 00:15:51.419
and brush their teeth,

00:15:51.419 --> 00:15:53.276
maybe read them a story and they lie bed

00:15:53.276 --> 00:15:54.109
and they fall asleep,

00:15:54.109 --> 00:15:56.350
that kind of regular nighttime routine

00:15:56.350 --> 00:15:58.916
at least for me starts to signal that okay

00:15:58.916 --> 00:15:59.874
it's time to wind down,

00:15:59.874 --> 00:16:02.130
it's time to stop thinking about YouTube

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.947
and entrepreneurs and all the stuff

00:16:03.947 --> 00:16:06.228
that can easily keep
me awake all night long

00:16:06.228 --> 00:16:07.397
'cause I'm excited.

00:16:07.397 --> 00:16:09.722
And so my nightly routine looks like this,

00:16:09.722 --> 00:16:11.160
usually around 10 o'clock now,

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:13.893
well me and my wife will
take Teemo, our puppy,

00:16:13.893 --> 00:16:15.683
out for a walk, half hour walk,

00:16:15.683 --> 00:16:18.300
we come back, we take
the stairs up, 13 flights

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:20.567
so that we lost some energy

00:16:20.567 --> 00:16:22.165
then we'll get ready for bed.

00:16:22.165 --> 00:16:25.404
I'll make sure that all the
lights are dim in the condo

00:16:25.404 --> 00:16:27.431
so there's nothing super
bright and in our face,

00:16:27.431 --> 00:16:28.780
even going to the bathroom,

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:30.101
only the shower light is on

00:16:30.101 --> 00:16:31.734
instead of the main overhead light

00:16:31.734 --> 00:16:33.613
'cause the big lights keep you awake.

00:16:33.613 --> 00:16:35.288
In our bedroom I'll just
have the nightlight on

00:16:35.288 --> 00:16:37.245
instead of the overhead
lights for the same reason.

00:16:37.245 --> 00:16:40.062
I'll brush my teeth, I'll
floss, I'll go to bed

00:16:40.062 --> 00:16:43.169
and usually I'll sit up for a
few minutes reading the book.

00:16:43.169 --> 00:16:45.201
I've got a book by my bedside,

00:16:45.201 --> 00:16:47.328
I'll read 10 to 20 pages of that book,

00:16:47.328 --> 00:16:50.317
put my sleeping mask on, and go to sleep.

00:16:50.317 --> 00:16:51.909
And that regular routine

00:16:51.909 --> 00:16:55.429
starting from 10 o'clock to
usually we're in bed by 11

00:16:55.429 --> 00:16:58.180
and I'm reading 'til maybe 11:15, 11:20,

00:16:58.180 --> 00:16:59.013
somewhere around there

00:16:59.013 --> 00:16:59.922
and that's when I go to sleep.

00:16:59.922 --> 00:17:02.470
That hour 15, hour 20,

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:04.387
slowly unwinds my body,

00:17:05.504 --> 00:17:07.437
slowly unwinds whatever stress

00:17:07.437 --> 00:17:09.129
or excitement was happening that day.

00:17:09.129 --> 00:17:11.699
It gives me a chance
to connect with my wife

00:17:11.699 --> 00:17:13.540
and just is a signal that okay

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:14.590
now it's time to go to sleep.

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:15.692
And so I think for you,

00:17:15.692 --> 00:17:17.166
establishing a nighttime routine

00:17:17.166 --> 00:17:19.102
that's more than just five minutes

00:17:19.102 --> 00:17:21.417
will really help you in unwinding

00:17:21.417 --> 00:17:23.009
what was happening during the day

00:17:23.009 --> 00:17:27.075
and set you up for a great night's sleep.

00:17:27.075 --> 00:17:28.869
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:17:28.869 --> 00:17:31.596
I made this video because
Robert Berrier asked me to.

00:17:31.596 --> 00:17:32.687
So if there's a topic
you'd like me to cover

00:17:32.687 --> 00:17:34.691
in the next edition of Believe Life,

00:17:34.691 --> 00:17:35.975
please leave it down in the comments below

00:17:35.975 --> 00:17:37.451
and we'll see what we can do.

00:17:37.451 --> 00:17:38.284
I'd also love to know,

00:17:38.284 --> 00:17:39.720
which rule did you like the best?

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.698
What did you learn the most
from watching this video?

00:17:41.698 --> 00:17:43.363
What are you going to take from it

00:17:43.363 --> 00:17:45.298
that you will immediately apply somewhere

00:17:45.298 --> 00:17:46.629
in your life or your business.

00:17:46.629 --> 00:17:47.636
I'd love to learn

00:17:47.636 --> 00:17:49.700
and please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.016
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout out to Doru Tarita.

00:17:52.016 --> 00:17:53.056
Thank you so much, Doru,

00:17:53.056 --> 00:17:55.291
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word.

00:17:55.291 --> 00:17:57.193
It really really means a lot to me

00:17:57.193 --> 00:17:58.543
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:17:58.543 --> 00:17:59.961
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:17:59.961 --> 00:18:01.689
I hope you have an amazing day.

00:18:01.689 --> 00:18:02.972
I believe in you,

00:18:02.972 --> 00:18:05.032
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:18:05.032 --> 00:18:08.119
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:18:08.119 --> 00:18:09.619
I'll see you soon.

00:18:16.005 --> 00:18:18.419
- I'm the author of The Sleep Revolution:

00:18:18.419 --> 00:18:21.498
Transforming Your Life
One Night at a Time.

00:18:21.498 --> 00:18:25.572
I collapsed from sleep
deprivation and burnout

00:18:25.572 --> 00:18:27.072
nine years ago now

00:18:27.995 --> 00:18:31.184
and that is what started me looking around

00:18:31.184 --> 00:18:33.398
and seeing that I was not alone.

00:18:33.398 --> 00:18:37.119
Millions of people are
suffering from burnout

00:18:37.119 --> 00:18:39.827
and that we've reached the point

00:18:39.827 --> 00:18:43.224
in our culture of glamorizing
sleep deprivation.

00:18:43.224 --> 00:18:47.157
And yet all the modern
science made it very clear

00:18:47.157 --> 00:18:48.512
that's what started me

00:18:48.512 --> 00:18:50.762
both changing my own habits

00:18:51.724 --> 00:18:56.570
and introducing a sleep
section at the Huffington Post

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.696
and bringing to our
readers and our viewers

00:18:59.696 --> 00:19:00.875
as I'm doing in the book

00:19:00.875 --> 00:19:04.545
all the latest science around sleep.

00:19:04.545 --> 00:19:06.967
Cultures often believe the wrong thing,

00:19:06.967 --> 00:19:08.425
I mean in the '50s,

00:19:08.425 --> 00:19:12.235
we used to think that
smoking was glamorous

00:19:12.235 --> 00:19:15.468
and we had doctors advertising cigarettes

00:19:15.468 --> 00:19:16.977
literally in white coats

00:19:16.977 --> 00:19:18.849
saying I smoke menthols

00:19:18.849 --> 00:19:20.879
because they refresh my throat.

00:19:20.879 --> 00:19:25.522
I feel that sleep deprivation
is the new smoking

00:19:25.522 --> 00:19:27.822
and we glamorize it,

00:19:27.822 --> 00:19:31.266
have men wearing it like a
badge of honor and women too.

00:19:31.266 --> 00:19:34.766
But men really see it as a virility symbol

00:19:35.817 --> 00:19:39.118
and brag about how they only
got four or five hours sleep

00:19:39.118 --> 00:19:42.612
which only works if you
have a genetic mutation.

00:19:42.612 --> 00:19:45.601
Sleep is not negotiable,
it's not optional.

00:19:45.601 --> 00:19:48.702
It is the foundation of a healthy,

00:19:48.702 --> 00:19:50.869
productive and happy life.

